% performance-note: No source performance exists. Beverly Glenn-Copeland's recording is a listening reference only, not a performance model to reproduce.
% composition-series: CBC Modern Canadian Bridges — pilot
% composition-grade: JK
% composition-elements: steady pulse; two contrasting note lengths; high and low; soft and medium dynamics; humming, breath and classroom-instrument timbres; four-bar question-and-answer form
% composition-listen: Beverly Glenn-Copeland — "Ever New", from Keyboard Fantasies (1986), as linked by the artist's official Bandcamp
% composition-listen-url: https://beverlyglenn-copeland.bandcamp.com/track/ever-new-2
% composition-bridge: Hear how a patient pulse, repeated shapes, warm voice and spacious keyboard colour can make very little material feel continually alive.
% composition-context: Beverly Glenn-Copeland is a Canadian composer and singer whose electronic and acoustic work found a new international audience decades after Keyboard Fantasies was recorded.
% composition-distance: This four-bar C-D humming game uses neither the reference melody, words, harmony nor keyboard pattern; it borrows only the general questions "what changes slowly?" and "how can timbre create space?"
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"1-2","role":"child conductor","prompt":"Choose the colour of the hum","choices":["closed-mouth hum","open oo","one soft chime"],"replyBars":2}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"3-4","role":"child conductor","prompt":"Choose how the answer arrives","choices":["grow louder","grow softer","stay still"],"replyBars":2}
% dossier-function: original JK listening-bridge and sound-choice game; an adult sings or plays the four bars while one child chooses the sound colour for the question and another chooses the dynamic shape of the answer
% dossier-elements: four bars in 4/4 at quarter note = 64; only C4 and D4; bars 1 and 3 use two half notes, bars 2 and 4 use one whole note; C4-D4 major second range; question-answer form 2+2 bars; dynamics and timbre are supplied by the live choices rather than fixed notation
% dossier-practice: Rehearsal key C; written range C4-D4. Keep the pulse in a slow rocking motion. Offer visual cards for hum, oo and chime, plus louder, softer and still. Participation may be vocal, instrumental, gestural or listening-only; never require a child to sing solo.
% dossier-accompaniment: No automatic band. Optional adult keyboard holds a very quiet C-G open fifth for all four bars; a single chime may replace the voice when selected. The sparse texture is newly authored and is not an arrangement of "Ever New."
% dossier-lyrics: Wordless on purpose: "mm" may become "oo." The absence of fixed words makes timbre, contour, breath and conversational choice the lesson.
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026. It is not by Beverly Glenn-Copeland and is not a simplified or transcribed version of "Ever New."
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0 for the melody, choice design, accompaniment suggestion and this ABC encoding. "Ever New" and its recording remain under their owners' copyright.
% dossier-respect: Introduce Glenn-Copeland by name and pronouns used in the artist's current materials; discuss the career and music without reducing the artist to identity labels. Do not call this classroom miniature ambient, new age or Glenn-Copeland's style; call it a listening bridge.
% dossier-review: Kindergarten music teacher, early-years/accessibility reviewer, second score reader and student listening trial; all reviews still required
